				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "xNZI/4+dDQC/o+CfA8ExpA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "Finally, finally we get to have a snippet type that actually knows how to execute code!"
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2022-06-11T18:30:14.076728+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:45:39.487355+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "t3BjTpCdDQCAUTe6A8ExpA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "With LanguageLink, this is where you would write an application strategy that holds on to your application. With a client of our own there is nothing to hold on to, so all of the work happens in the coder model."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2022-06-11T18:31:19.494771+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:45:39.487355+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "E6NbUZCdDQCAU8nsA8ExpA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "The coder model is responsible for taking source code from the snippet, handing it over to the server, and giving GT something to display. As such, it is the main link between the IDE and the execution engine."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2022-06-11T18:32:07.688865+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:45:39.487355+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "ypstVZCdDQCAYOo/A8ExpA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "The method that does this is {{gtMethod:GtCarpCoderModel>>#primitiveEvaluate:inContext:onFailDo:|expanded}}. Evaluate and Inspect both end up there, so implementing it is enough to make both buttons work."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2022-06-11T18:34:40.493148+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:45:39.487355+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "WALsXZCdDQCBUGyRA8ExpA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "It is not a big method. It sends the source to the client along with the snippet’s session, and answers whatever the result wants to be inspected as. A cell that doesn’t compile still answers a result, with the diagnostics in it; the failure block is only for a server that can’t be reached."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
				"createEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"createTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2022-06-11T18:36:42.952545+02:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-09-26T21:45:39.487355+02:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "R7ljZZCdDQCBUlkLA8ExpA=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "`preferredInspectable` is where it gets interesting. To start with, it is fine to answer the {{gtClass:CarpEvalResult}} itself, and in gt4carp that is still what happens when a cell has no value. When it has one, the result answers a live object instead, which is the topic of [[Live values]] in the second part."
			}
		]
